@@ -0,0 +1,121 @@
+---
+title: Generated widgets (experimental)
+description: Typed JSX components generated from Flutter constructors with fuse add and fuse gen
+---
+
+> **Experimental.** This is a prototype on the `spike/e2e` branch. Commands, file names and the manifest format will change.
+
+`fuse gen` reads Flutter widget constructors with the Dart analyzer and writes both halves of a widget for you: a Dart adapter that decodes props, and a typed JSX component. You do not write `FuseNode` code and you do not write a props interface.
+
+It works on three kinds of input:
+
+| Input | Where you declare it | Import from |
+|-------|----------------------|-------------|
+| Flutter SDK widgets | `native.flutter` in `fuse.config.ts` | `~/_generated/flutter` |
+| pub.dev packages | `fuse add <package>` (writes `native.packages`) | `~/_generated/<package>` |
+| Your own Flutter widgets | any widget class in `dart/lib/fuse/*.dart` | `~/_generated/app` |
+
+## Add a pub.dev package
+
+```bash
+fuse add percent_indicator
+```
+
+This adds the dependency to `dart/pubspec.yaml`, records the package under `native.packages` in `fuse.config.ts`, generates the bindings and prints what the package offers, including every parameter that could not be bridged and why.
+
+```tsx
+import { CircularPercentIndicator } from "~/_generated/percent_indicator";
+
+<CircularPercentIndicator radius={40} lineWidth={6} percent={progress()} progressColor="#3366ff" />
+```
+
+A new pub package is new native code, so the app needs a native rebuild.
+
+## Allow Flutter SDK widgets
+
+```ts title="fuse.config.ts"
+export default defineConfig({
+  native: {
+    flutter: ["ListTile", "Switch", "Slider", "AppBar", "Scaffold", "Image.network"],
+    packages: { percent_indicator: {} },
+  },
+});
+```
+
+Run `fuse gen` after editing the list. Only listed widgets are generated, because each one adds to the binary. Named constructors are separate components (`Image.network` becomes `ImageNetwork`).
+
+Several names collide with solid-fuse and Solid exports (`Text`, `Stack`, `Icon`, `Switch`), so a namespace import reads best:
+
+```tsx
+import * as F from "~/_generated/flutter";
+
+<F.ListTile title={<Text>Boost</Text>} trailing={<F.Switch value={on()} onChanged={setOn} />} />
+```
+
+## Write a plain Flutter widget
+
+Put an ordinary widget in `dart/lib/fuse/`. Typed constructor parameters are the whole contract:
+
+```dart title="dart/lib/fuse/rating_badge.dart"
+class RatingBadge extends StatelessWidget {
+  const RatingBadge({super.key, required this.label, this.stars = 0, this.onRated, this.child});
+  final String label;
+  final int stars;
+  final ValueChanged<int>? onRated;
+  final Widget? child;
+  // build() as usual
+}
+```
+
+```tsx
+import { RatingBadge } from "~/_generated/app";
+
+<RatingBadge label="Rating" stars={stars()} onRated={setStars} />
+```
+
+`fuse dev` and `fuse build` notice when these files change and regenerate.
+
+## How Dart types map to props
+
+| Dart | JSX prop |
+|------|----------|
+| `bool`, `int`, `double`, `String` | `boolean`, `number`, `string` |
+| `enum` | the value name as a string: `"round"` |
+| `Widget child` / `List<Widget> children` | JSX children |
+| other `Widget` parameters (`title`, `leading`) | a JSX element: `title={<Text>Hi</Text>}` |
+| `List<Widget>` parameters (`actions`) | an array of JSX elements |
+| `void Function(A)` | `(a) => void`; payload classes become plain objects |
+| a class with constructors | an object keyed by parameter name; `$` picks a named constructor or subclass: `{ $: "symmetric", horizontal: 8 }` |
+| static constants | a string: `"Colors.teal"`, `"Curves.easeInOut"`, `"center"` |
+| `Color`, `EdgeInsets`, `BorderRadius`, `Duration`, `FontWeight` | also accept the short forms `"#3366ff"`, `16`, `12`, `300`, `700` |
+
+Builders, callbacks that return a value, and delegate objects cannot cross the bridge (the JS thread answers asynchronously). Those parameters are left out and listed; a widget that requires one is not generated. Use a hand-written [`FuseNode` builder](/docs/extending/creating-widgets) for those.
+
+## What gets written
+
+```
+fuse.lock                                 resolved versions and manifest hashes
+src/_generated/<unit>.manifest.json       every widget, prop, type, default and doc string
+src/_generated/<unit>.ts                  typed components
+src/_generated/NATIVE.md                  index of available widgets (for people and agents)
+src/_generated/native.catalog.json        the same, machine-readable
+dart/lib/_generated/<unit>.fuse.g.dart    Dart adapters
+dart/lib/_generated/fuse_generated.dart   registration, called from fuse_packages.dart
+```
+
+Commit all of it. `fuse gen --check` exits non-zero when the output is stale, for CI. `fuse catalog <Widget>` prints one widget's props, defaults and docs.
+
+## Dev-mode validation
+
+In `fuse dev`, props are checked against the manifest before they are sent to Dart. This catches values TypeScript cannot see (`any`, spreads, data from JSON):
+
+```
+[fuse] <percent_indicator.LinearPercentIndicator> in <Broken>: unknown prop "lineHeigth". Did you mean "lineHeight"?
+[fuse] <app.RatingBadge> in <Broken>: prop "tone" expects BadgeTone (neutral | positive | negative), got "positiv". Did you mean "positive"?
+```
+
+Release bundles contain neither the validator nor the manifests.
+
+## Upgrades
+
+`fuse.lock` records the Flutter version, each package version and a hash of each manifest. When `fuse dev` or `fuse build` sees a difference it regenerates and prints the API changes. A parameter that was removed upstream becomes a TypeScript error at the place you used it.
